# Commit yourself

When a coherent unit of work is done and a commit is warranted — **commit it yourself**. Do not leave “should I commit?” or wait for an explicit “commit” unless the user said not to, or the change is still exploratory / half-baked.

## When to commit

- A bugfix, feature slice, or refactor that builds and is ready to keep
- After the related rebuild/tests you already ran for that work
- Include new/updated project rules that belong with the change

## When not to

- User said not to commit, or is mid-design / still iterating on the same WIP
- Only scratch, secrets, or generated junk (`build/`, local logs, credentials)
- Empty or purely cosmetic churn with no meaningful intent

## How

Follow the repo’s normal git safety protocol: status + diff + log style, stage only the relevant files, concise message focused on why, no amend/force/hooks-skip unless explicitly requested. Report the commit hash briefly when done.
